WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:02.442
SPEAKER 1: Building cloud-native
applications can often

00:00:02.442 --> 00:00:04.590
be difficult. Setting up
the right environments,

00:00:04.590 --> 00:00:07.710
accessing client libraries,
and figuring out an easy way

00:00:07.710 --> 00:00:11.172
to deploy and debug your
application is time consuming.

00:00:11.172 --> 00:00:12.630
To help you solve
these challenges,

00:00:12.630 --> 00:00:14.190
we built Cloud Code.

00:00:14.190 --> 00:00:16.320
Cloud Code is a
set of IDE plugins

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:18.600
that allows you to
write, deploy, and debug

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:20.835
cloud-native applications
quickly and easily.

00:00:20.835 --> 00:00:23.190
Cloud Code supports
a number of IDEs,

00:00:23.190 --> 00:00:25.590
including Visual Studio
Code, JetBrains IDEs

00:00:25.590 --> 00:00:27.870
such as IntelliJ, and
Cloud Shell Editor.

00:00:27.870 --> 00:00:30.390
This way, you can leverage
the tools you already know.

00:00:30.390 --> 00:00:33.330
Let's dive deeper into how Cloud
Code helps you write, deploy,

00:00:33.330 --> 00:00:35.140
and debug your code faster.

00:00:35.140 --> 00:00:37.230
First, a collection of
starter templates showing

00:00:37.230 --> 00:00:39.270
best practices in a
variety of languages

00:00:39.270 --> 00:00:41.040
are available out
of the box, and you

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:42.870
can run these on Google
Kubernetes engine

00:00:42.870 --> 00:00:44.880
or Cloud Run with
just a few clicks.

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:47.670
As you build out your app, you
can easily enable and integrate

00:00:47.670 --> 00:00:50.280
numerous Google Cloud
APIs from your IDE.

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:52.440
You can also leverage
Cloud Code's Secret Manager

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:55.560
integration to access and
manage sensitive data, like API

00:00:55.560 --> 00:00:57.690
keys, passwords,
and certificates

00:00:57.690 --> 00:00:58.860
right from your IDE.

00:00:58.860 --> 00:01:01.500
And if you aren't someone
who loves writing YAML files,

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:03.810
Cloud Code improves your
YAML authoring experience

00:01:03.810 --> 00:01:06.863
with features like Code Snippets
and In-line Documentation.

00:01:06.863 --> 00:01:08.280
Once you're done
writing your code

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:10.920
and those pesky YAML
files, it's time to deploy.

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:13.230
If you're trying to run
your Kubernetes application

00:01:13.230 --> 00:01:15.180
on a local Kubernetes
cluster, Cloud Code

00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:17.130
makes it easy to deploy
to Docker Desktop

00:01:17.130 --> 00:01:19.410
or will create and start
a minikube cluster for you

00:01:19.410 --> 00:01:20.550
if you don't have one.

00:01:20.550 --> 00:01:23.220
When developing, Cloud Code
can automatically rebuild

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:25.710
and redeploy your code every
time you make a change.

00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:28.110
It even supports hot
reloading individual files

00:01:28.110 --> 00:01:30.780
that change to the container
without a rebuild or redeploy,

00:01:30.780 --> 00:01:32.910
so you see updates
in under a second.

00:01:32.910 --> 00:01:35.700
Even better, Cloud Code works
with any Kubernetes cluster,

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:38.370
be it on Google Cloud
or any other cloud.

00:01:38.370 --> 00:01:40.320
After deploying, you
may discover issues

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:41.520
in your application.

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:44.010
Cloud Code is here to help by
providing the same debugging

00:01:44.010 --> 00:01:46.110
experience you're
used to in the IDE,

00:01:46.110 --> 00:01:48.150
even when debugging a
remote Kubernetes cluster

00:01:48.150 --> 00:01:49.920
or a serverless application.

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:52.140
Just place breakpoints,
then debug your code

00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:55.350
in your Kubernetes cluster or
locally on Cloud Run emulator.

00:01:55.350 --> 00:01:56.970
If you need to dive
deeper, Cloud Code

00:01:56.970 --> 00:01:58.740
also comes with a
built-in log viewer

00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:00.510
to help you monitor
and debug issues

00:02:00.510 --> 00:02:02.670
with your Kubernetes
and Cloud Run services

00:02:02.670 --> 00:02:04.140
right within your IDE.

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:05.670
Now you know how
Cloud Code works

00:02:05.670 --> 00:02:07.200
to improve your
experience creating

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:09.780
cloud-native applications
within your favorite IDE

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:12.960
by helping you write, deploy,
and debug your code faster.

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:14.880
We hope this short
introduction to Cloud Code

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:16.710
gives you all you
need to get started.

00:02:16.710 --> 00:02:18.270
Cloud Code is the
most productive way

00:02:18.270 --> 00:02:19.920
to build cloud-native
applications.

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:24.330
And you can give it a try by
going to cloud.google.com/code.

00:02:24.330 --> 00:02:26.640
Also, you can reach
out to us via Slack.

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:29.500
You can find us using this link
with #cloud-code as the channel

00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:30.000
name.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:32.450
[MUSIC PLAYING]

